From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: the GPU's "mem" clock is PLL_GPU0, not the MBUS gate

drm/powervr takes three clocks by name -- "core" (required), "sys" and
"mem" (both optional) -- and does nothing with them but
clk_prepare_enable() and disable. It never sets a rate: pvr_device.c has
no clk_set_rate and no OPP or devfreq use at all, checked in the tree we
build, not assumed.

`arm64-dts-a733-add-the-gpu-node-disabled` wired "mem" to CLK_MBUS_GPU0. The
tree where this driver actually binds on this SoC and renders -- a 6.18.19
kernel built from Alex Cao's A733 BSP port for Linux 6.18
(https://github.com/alexcaoys/allwinner-bsp, branch linux-6.18.y), the
kernel behind the Fedora GPU desktop of 2026-06-25 -- wires it to
CLK_PLL_GPU0 in configs/linux-6.18/sun60iw2p1.dtsi (the GPU node has been there
since the file was added in commit b48ce2c542c5, Link below):

	clocks = <&ccu CLK_GPU0>, <&ccu CLK_PLL_GPU0>, <&ccu CLK_BUS_GPU0>;
	clock-names = "core", "mem", "sys";

So on that tree, binding the driver turns the GPU PLL on. On ours it
opens an MBUS master gate instead, and PLL_GPU0 is never enabled by
anybody: PLL_GPU0_CTRL reads 0x41104500 on our image, PLL_EN (bit 31)
clear and LOCK (bit 28) clear (field names from
the A733 user manual, offset 0x00E0).

This aligns the property with that tree. It does not touch the operating
point: `arm64-dts-a733-enable-the-gpu-at-the-vendor-s-operating-point`'s reasoning stands unchanged -- GPUPLL cannot serve 600 MHz,
its VCO floor is above the required 1200 MHz, so CLK_GPU0 keeps
PLL_PERI0_600M as its assigned parent. "mem" here is a clock the driver
switches on, not a parent.

What is deliberately given up, because a port has to say what it drops:
CLK_MBUS_GPU0 leaves the node, so nothing claims the GPU's MBUS master
gate and clk_disable_unused() closes it. Measured on the 8 GiB board on 2026-09-07:
without clk_ignore_unused, MBUS_MAT_CLK_GATING bit 16 reads 0 on our
image and 1 on the vendor stack, and setting it by hand moves nothing --
neither PWSR nor anything else observable. The reference tree does not
name it either. If the GPU ever needs it as a master, it needs a claim
that survives, and that is a separate patch with its own proof.

⚠ Motivation, stated plainly so it is not mistaken for a fix: GPU_CORE
does not power up on our stack and does on the vendor's, and this is the
last documented difference left between our GPU node and the node in a
tree where the same driver brings the same GPU up. It is a candidate, not
a cause. The falsifier is one line: after boot with this applied,
GPU_CORE PWSR reads 0x8 instead of 0x0. If it still reads 0x0, the
difference is elsewhere and this patch is only a correctness fix for the
"mem" name.

Link: https://github.com/alexcaoys/allwinner-bsp/commit/b48ce2c542c57940febe0f7587d2cf79a1dcca12
Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -2781,7 +2781,7 @@
 			compatible = "img,img-bxm-4-64", "img,img-rogue";
 			reg = <0x0 0x01800000 0x0 0x90000>;
 			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 63 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
-			clocks = <&ccu CLK_GPU0>, <&ccu CLK_MBUS_GPU0>,
+			clocks = <&ccu CLK_GPU0>, <&ccu CLK_PLL_GPU0>,
 				 <&ccu CLK_BUS_GPU0>;
 			clock-names = "core", "mem", "sys";
 			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_GPU0>;
